{"Once upon a time there were four little Rabbits, and their names were— Flopsy, Mopsy, Cotton-tail, and Peter. They lived with their Mother in a sand-bank, underneath the root of a very big fir-tree.":"7dc0af89f15e75959a7af0deaec9c738bce744c99f2fb00378cee65c6bb7e748","“Now my dears,” said old Mrs. Rabbit one morning, “you may go into the fields or down the lane, but don't go into Mr. McGregor's garden: your Father had an accident there; he was put in a pie by Mrs. McGregor.”":"67b9c64e4202d718a324c4878821d55b6fba65a8a80a9155b1750d07162decde","“Now run along, and don't get into mischief. I am going out.”":"a15d330b6b617e4af0bd0719395880ede48577eef93f5e7936e3c39a6b5318d8","Then old Mrs. Rabbit took a basket and her umbrella, and went through the wood to the baker's. She bought a loaf of brown bread and five currant buns.":"8089a33399f42998fca84a9d972e499ccf11fe1f91202e5a3e93a92836899ad7","Flopsy, Mopsy, and Cotton-tail, who were good little bunnies, went down the lane to gather blackberries.":"15711db3cc782b6a76f6ad6a3543ee22c7a6f4f44662dc57306411c5c19cd43f","But Peter, who was very naughty, ran straight away to Mr. McGregor's garden, and squeezed under the gate!":"c6f40eb0e681c8ff07b78f928ab9b9938e81dcaba50b88a3f57976fa8a5f85e8","First he ate some lettuces and some French beans; and then he ate some radishes;":"d16c4e3fa40ba78c6a7991984524c92aab0fe99e84dbe354201164d9cc232e27","And then, feeling rather sick, he went to look for some parsley.":"99c0200cc9c44442a49a6d8549685366a6809aec32cdcb3742ed78399c1718e4","But round the end of a cucumber frame, whom should he meet but Mr. McGregor!":"338c4474c182a7fa449e74aa0b31a99ef6a1b1bfe670b000b8f62f9e829c9dc8","Mr. McGregor was on his hands and knees planting out young cabbages, but he jumped up and ran after Peter, waving a rake and calling out, “Stop thief!”":"588613bc83a2dd2c9cc5eaa94e1d0c1937783acf78550caf82a951ddce17e34a","Peter was most dreadfully frightened; he rushed all over the garden, for he had forgotten the way back to the gate. He lost one of his shoes among the cabbages, and the other shoe amongst the potatoes.":"a9a7a7031eca105b2d75ae435b5d96c84a6e0ea0afb347bbae8e95f32e93de9f","After losing them, he ran on four legs and went faster, so that I think he might have got away altogether if he had not unfortunately run into a gooseberry net, and got caught by the large buttons on his jacket. It was a blue jacket with brass buttons, quite new.":"3a49b213cb6966ad0c8b1beadbde4f7d89cd733fc1176c07fb7f1b1d7d313b9c","Peter gave himself up for lost, and shed big tears; but his sobs were overheard by some friendly sparrows, who flew to him in great excitement, and implored him to exert himself.":"fdaa82353806d7f97d8c13cbd1073ee3da8e8252e288eb1f42b1045983a9f691","Mr. McGregor came up with a sieve, which he intended to pop upon the top of Peter; but Peter wriggled out just in time, leaving his jacket behind him.":"0968a96c0bb0b46efa7f83f80aca9a392622c9759e9db6fdbbc50b5da2892c03","And rushed into the tool-shed, and jumped into a can. It would have been a beautiful thing to hide in, if it had not had so much water in it.":"0a62e42746a6f223345fc61240ac2f49e7ab42f3e0606db104b4a05f4c3f671d","Mr. McGregor was quite sure that Peter was somewhere in the tool-shed, perhaps hidden underneath a flower-pot. He began to turn them over carefully, looking under each. Presently Peter sneezed—“Kertyschoo!” Mr. McGregor was after him in no time.":"1e77dd98a17dbda7cdf72f4f07610d4063f2181283d2b8f2a9991df02848d713","And tried to put his foot upon Peter, who jumped out of a window, upsetting three plants. The window was too small for Mr. McGregor, and he was tired of running after Peter. He went back to his work.":"48b6bb6d90ec4cb69d574f08bd332ab8427c2647b1e9d3fba07f2bbd7b5615b3","Peter sat down to rest; he was out of breath and trembling with fright, and he had not the least idea which way to go. Also he was very damp with sitting in that can. After a time he began to wander about, going lippity—lippity—not very fast, and looking all round.":"1100a602463a9aed1d6e849772facef3010b87babb01c3cf0ca0d5f5f2cfd1bc","He found a door in a wall; but it was locked, and there was no room for a fat little rabbit to squeeze underneath. An old mouse was running in and out over the stone doorstep, carrying peas and beans to her family in the wood. Peter asked her the way to the gate, but she had such a large pea in her mouth that she could not answer. She only shook her head at him. Peter began to cry.":"38f70b5e1fe64b857ea5ca949b32e6be45c7e35c54aa7034dead0ab13fe10ee1","Then he tried to find his way straight across the garden, but he became more and more puzzled. Presently, he came to a pond where Mr. McGregor filled his water-cans. A white cat was staring at some gold-fish, she sat very, very still, but now and then the tip of her tail twitched as if it were alive. Peter thought it best to go away without speaking to her; he had heard about cats from his cousin, little Benjamin Bunny.":"d9c2ecdf029954c2fa603d25402c58760dc2356fe14a592e893d0c34f09876fd","He went back towards the tool-shed, but suddenly, quite close to him, he heard the noise of a hoe—scr-r-ritch, scratch, scratch, scritch. Peter scuttered underneath the bushes. But presently, as nothing happened, he came out, and climbed upon a wheelbarrow and peeped over. The first thing he saw was Mr. McGregor hoeing onions. His back was turned towards Peter, and beyond him was the gate!":"bbd4a1b08290a2ee0974839200104bd0fdbaea138069b36b273cceb503102aba","Peter got down very quietly off the wheelbarrow; and started running as fast as he could go, along a straight walk behind some black-currant bushes. Mr. McGregor caught sight of him at the corner, but Peter did not care. He slipped underneath the gate, and was safe at last in the wood outside the garden.":"0fb65b523b8cf1ddfbf4731e4b5ef4926bba4d9196490ece41b15465bbf32346","Mr. McGregor hung up the little jacket and the shoes for a scare-crow to frighten the blackbirds. Peter never stopped running or looked behind him till he got home to the big fir-tree.":"3040c0928e64c949d3f18d1c3f8fe0acd59f74683fc21fa4c491b10d3e374a7e","He was so tired that he flopped down upon the nice soft sand on the floor of the rabbit-hole and shut his eyes. His mother was busy cooking; she wondered what he had done with his clothes. It was the second little jacket and pair of shoes that Peter had lost in a fortnight!":"d1c11cc22067d301450d0247217e4842b8da2d211c4f4aac4aab3968bb2ab6a2","I am sorry to say that Peter was not very well during the evening. His mother put him to bed, and made some camomile tea; and she gave a dose of it to Peter! ‘One table-spoonful to be taken at bed-time.’":"9fc608077feeb4cfc2ead208c43f880616704e7c31626e54ea67ba606dedaef5","But Flopsy, Mopsy, and Cotton-tail had bread and milk and blackberries for supper. THE END":"2dee0774c6d7b52dd0ebba0f039451699105e0fbb5f17ac997ddf0c5d4e9bd98","Long ago, all the people on earth were one big family. They lived together and spoke only one language. Everyone could understand everyone else.":"a744f38e5754b4a813af9e328bad35934ec8f3c3a5f286583ac16943f4ccadf9","As the people travelled toward the east, they came to a wide, flat land called Shinar. “Let us stay here,” they said. And so this plain became their home.":"ea63f2ae80e0cee74e6660a87b6fb7eb614857e7ea44143bbc27e56473244c9d","“Come,” they said to one another, “let us make bricks and bake them hard in the fire.” They used bricks in the place of stone, and sticky tar to hold them together.":"c6d296423a5fce3f25fcd20d27d74e0a8d6d0f0c3e75d0c3984d679af34a1f10","Then the people dreamed a great dream. “Let us build a city, and a tower so tall that its very top reaches heaven! Then we will make a great name for ourselves, and we will never be scattered across the earth.”":"c1df7149b5dba67f513f0e683c54f00852a85d9a8d5f3895e659ae288f88c127","The tower grew higher and higher. Then the Lord came down to look at the city and the tower that the people were building. He saw that they were all one, with one language, and that nothing they planned to do would be too hard for them.":"3855708888982dc9f18b1222a3767e6f1a35f32e9d5958b7e00d31f71a44f2b8","So the Lord said, “Let us go down and mix up their language, so that they can no longer understand one another.” Suddenly the builders heard strange new words. No one could understand his friend!":"4b1eb897def20e7982a583869a7c898a1555cef548235f5d64b80a1fadad970e","The people could no longer work together, so they stopped building the city. They left the great tower unfinished, and spread out over the face of all the earth.":"a1dfe94722cf8244cccbb181c36a7fdffe8b8df4596e551b86c519eaca10a6d6","And that is why the city was called <b>Babel</b> — a name that sounds like the word for “confusion.” There the Lord mixed up the one language of the whole world, and from there its people spread across the earth.":"f1a8e2aa1f3ea44a41227cf2d8cbad405e4407177876a7c12393462e083c40d4","After Jesus had returned to heaven, his followers stayed together, praying and waiting as he had told them to. On the morning of a feast called Pentecost, they were all in one room, of one heart and one mind.":"d2be1679acd3ff2849a6b76b6e4e0374bc7c2d09d63aac106cd334b05670ce47","Suddenly, without any warning, a sound came from the sky — like a strong, rushing wind. It filled the whole house, roaring around them, though no window had been opened.":"517f83111e6cc7a1edfb2fd1b06169d04b83984925440169f277e52b6c0f5d42","Then something they had never seen appeared: little flames, shaped like tongues of fire, came to rest gently on the head of each one of them — glowing, but not burning.":"055e41d31533a2ada33db4e1d1183b071d626342fb912cca2f9bac06c99e4971","They were filled with God's Spirit, and they began to speak — but not in their own language. Each one found himself speaking in a language he had never learned, as the Spirit gave him the words.":"218bb49a047b4986dc8c8dfcfdd178b3b1c3fe7fdeec9614793633242016f6da","In Jerusalem that day were people from many faraway countries. A crowd gathered — and every single person was amazed, because each one could hear the friends speaking in his own home language.":"1f4d0722711ec853eac4c32dc9faf05516a522a960ae77976c9de8cd22ff5856","Long ago at Babel, one language became many, and the people were scattered apart. Now, with many languages, everyone understood — and the people were drawn together. What Babel had divided, this day began to heal.":"e45906bb9ba4e032ab2f2e94c01c0d5290e78cea0d994b8cfedbf2b9f8a3103b","On walls all over Egypt were rows of tiny pictures — birds, eyes, waves, little figures. Everyone could see them, but the meaning was lost. For fourteen hundred years, this was a language locked with no key.":"2a46585eba0cb2f8684dce55ddee6d4eecbf8f41ae9581a462cff2a599624695","In 1799, soldiers digging near a town called Rosetta struck something hard in the ground. It was a huge slab of dark stone, and every inch of one side was covered in careful rows of writing.":"81db440c37508b16df5441ad737b4aada0739825ea971b6f76b91d8c690bb25a","Scholars gathered round and saw something wonderful. The stone held the very same message written three times, in three different scripts — Egyptian hieroglyphs at the top, everyday Egyptian in the middle, and, at the bottom, Greek. And Greek, they could read!":"49b638da299431d7c3a09f27f57096e0c58dddad944c6d33f011a69d32a0b359","In France, a young man named Jean-François Champollion had loved languages since he was a boy. He taught himself script after script — Latin, Greek, Hebrew, Arabic, and more — dreaming that one day he would read the hieroglyphs no one else could.":"244c8f21db78dec745427dd87ab89bf042dbe27e5b93b5347cfb5482702f6317","Champollion noticed that some names — kings and queens — were drawn inside little ovals. He guessed that these signs might stand for <b>sounds</b>, like letters. Matching “Ptolemy” and “Cleopatra” sign by sign, sound by sound, the hieroglyphs slowly began to speak.":"ef4d9e18b79fe24ab2fed0a07dda4b83a3516b28c87935862f3ed917ad642608","One day in 1822, it all came together. The story says Champollion ran to his brother, cried out that he had done it, and fainted from the shock of joy. Egypt's lost language could be read again — and three thousand years of stories swung open like a door.":"b8ef654a5f5aa86a14cec8b2dd468b2c6deb58008671df12035c60068f6ac681","Helen could not see the faces of the people she loved, and could not hear their voices. She had thoughts and feelings, but no way to share them. When no one could understand her, she often grew angry and afraid, lost in the silent dark.":"794f3d445ab169de7d654768a155724a53f5c4ab0941be989e3efd74e4900dca","Then a teacher named Anne Sullivan came to live with her. Anne would take Helen's hand and, with quick finger movements, spell words into her palm — d-o-l-l, when she gave her a doll. Helen felt the shapes, but did not know they meant anything.":"68063ad736453b08cfee0bd2abf4a174aa55c00e2909e850e553e0c698909fb3","Helen learned to copy the finger-letters back, quick and clever, the way you might copy a game with your hands. But it was only a game to her. She still did not understand the great secret: that every thing in the world has a name.":"897f9e9524531c0c896095040fb1494827606b263adf567187e0ae955e50a4d1","One warm morning, Anne led Helen out to the well-house, where someone was pumping water. She held Helen's hand under the spout. As the cool water rushed over it, she spelled into the other hand the word <span>w-a-t-e-r</span> — first slowly, then again and again.":"775c040d992a4852d9a6938023f653c2ab75d6b285ad8a99b9345abb5d161c16","Suddenly, like a flash of light, Helen understood. The cool something rushing over her hand and the word spelled into her palm were the same thing — this was <span>water</span>! In that instant the secret of language opened, and her whole mind woke up.":"d100031285d674a17274fdfb1e37595fbc1112d5533ee6aef99f572a697b18b3","That day Helen learned the names of everything she touched, and the whole world seemed new. Words became her door out of the dark. She would grow up to read, write, go to college, and speak to people everywhere — all beginning with one word poured into her hand.":"7fec8b3441a14d4e1e3e01c83bf2ee116118893c04c1849ca098b49e39fe2973"}